      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2604.4,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 649,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than a simple chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the fundamental prerequisite for life as we know it. \n\nHere is a breakdown of water in all its dimensions:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater is unique because of how it behaves under normal Earth conditions:\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (water), **solid** (ice), and **gas** (steam or water vapor).\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it can easily carry nutrients, minerals, and chemicals through living organisms and ecosystems.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands* and becomes less dense. This is why ice floats on liquid water, which prevents lakes and oceans from freezing solid from the bottom up, allowing aquatic life to survive the winter.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat with only small changes in its own temperature. This helps regulate the climate of the Earth and the internal temperature of living things.\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nWater covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, primarily in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). \n* Only about **2.5% of Earth's water is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. \n* Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible freshwater in lakes, rivers, and the atmosphere.\n* Water is constantly moving in a continuous cycle called the **hydrological (water) cycle**: it evaporates from the surface, rises into the atmosphere, cools and condenses into clouds, and falls back to Earth as precipitation (rain or snow).\n\n### 3. Importance to Life\nWater is often called the \"elixir of life.\" Without it, biological life cannot exist:\n* **Human Body:** The human body is made up of roughly 60% water. It regulates body temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and acts as a medium for cellular processes.\n* **Ecosystems:** Water provides habitats for millions of species and drives the weather patterns that sustain terrestrial plants and animals.\n* **Agriculture:** Human civilization was built around water; we need it to grow crops, raise livestock, and sustain modern industry.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It shapes the geology of our planet, drives our weather, makes up the majority of our bodies, and connects all living things in a delicate, global web of survival."
